1f:T528,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BlogPosting","@id":"https://www.outbrick.site/blog/celeste-assist-mode-accessibility/#article","mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://www.outbrick.site/blog/celeste-assist-mode-accessibility"},"headline":"Celeste’s Assist Mode: accessibility as better design","description":"Optional support can preserve the heart of a challenge while giving more players a route into it.","image":["https://www.outbrick.site/blog/celeste-assist-mode-accessibility.png"],"datePublished":"2026-09-04","dateModified":"2026-09-04","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"OutBrick Editorial Desk","description":"The OutBrick Editorial Desk turns player research, accessibility practice, and game-making lessons into useful, readable notes."},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"OutBrick","url":"https://www.outbrick.site","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.outbrick.site/icon.png"}},"articleSection":"Inclusive design","keywords":"Celeste, Assist Mode, accessibility, difficulty design","citation":["https://doi.org/10.1145/3613904.3642804","https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/use-of-color","https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/uwp/gaming/accessibility-for-games"]}14:["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$1f"}}]
15:["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"BreadcrumbList\",\"itemListElement\":[{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"OutBrick\",\"item\":\"https://www.outbrick.site\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Journal\",\"item\":\"https://www.outbrick.site/blog\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":3,\"name\":\"Celeste’s Assist Mode: accessibility as better design\",\"item\":\"https://www.outbrick.site/blog/celeste-assist-mode-accessibility\"}]}"}}]
16:["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"FAQPage\",\"mainEntity\":[{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Does Assist Mode make Celeste easier?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"It lets the player adjust selected conditions of play. The important design idea is that the player chooses the route, rather than accessibility being treated as a separate game.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"What accessibility options does OutBrick have?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"OutBrick reinforces color with shapes and glyphs and includes a color-blind mode. We continue to treat player feedback and testing as part of the work.\"}}]}"}}]
17:["$","div",null,{"className":"article-body","children":[["$","section","the-core-can-stay-intact",{"id":"the-core-can-stay-intact","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"The core can stay intact"}],[["$","p","Celeste’s Assist Mode is compelling because it does not require the team to pretend that difficulty is meaningless. It gives players optional ways to change the conditions of play, so the mountain can remain a place of attention and discovery even when the default timing or speed is not a good fit.",{"children":"Celeste’s Assist Mode is compelling because it does not require the team to pretend that difficulty is meaningless. It gives players optional ways to change the conditions of play, so the mountain can remain a place of attention and discovery even when the default timing or speed is not a good fit."}],["$","p","The design distinction matters. A support option is not a second-class version of the game. It is an invitation to choose the conditions under which the player can participate in the idea the game cares about.",{"children":"The design distinction matters. A support option is not a second-class version of the game. It is an invitation to choose the conditions under which the player can participate in the idea the game cares about."}]],null,null,["$","p",null,{"className":"article-source-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"Research trail"}],[["$","a","microsoft-game-accessibility",{"href":"#reference-microsoft-game-accessibility","aria-label":"Jump to source: Microsoft (n.d.)","children":["[",3,"]"]}],["$","a","accessibility-hard-mode-2024",{"href":"#reference-accessibility-hard-mode-2024","aria-label":"Jump to source: Martinez, Froehlich, & Fogarty (2024)","children":["[",1,"]"]}]]]}]]}],["$","section","what-disabled-players-teach-designers",{"id":"what-disabled-players-teach-designers","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"What disabled players teach designers"}],[["$","p","Martinez, Froehlich, and Fogarty interviewed 13 gamers with disabilities about game adoption and described phases including discovery, evaluation, and adaptation. Their recommendations include stronger community resources, socially created access, customizable experiences, and support for unconventional play. The sample is small and qualitative, but its specificity is exactly the point: access needs show up in real decisions, not only in a checklist.",{"children":"Martinez, Froehlich, and Fogarty interviewed 13 gamers with disabilities about game adoption and described phases including discovery, evaluation, and adaptation. Their recommendations include stronger community resources, socially created access, customizable experiences, and support for unconventional play. The sample is small and qualitative, but its specificity is exactly the point: access needs show up in real decisions, not only in a checklist."}],["$","p","The research also challenges a narrow idea of mastery. A player may be deeply engaged while using a control remap, a slower speed, a visual cue, a guide, or a movement pattern the designer never imagined. The goal is participation with agency, not performance of the designer’s preferred route.",{"children":"The research also challenges a narrow idea of mastery. A player may be deeply engaged while using a control remap, a slower speed, a visual cue, a guide, or a movement pattern the designer never imagined. The goal is participation with agency, not performance of the designer’s preferred route."}]],null,null,"$L20"]}],"$L21","$L22"]}]
18:["$","section",null,{"id":"faq","className":"article-faq","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"blog-kicker","children":"Questions, answered"}],["$","h2",null,{"children":"A few useful answers"}],[["$","details","Does Assist Mode make Celeste easier?",{"children":[["$","summary",null,{"children":"Does Assist Mode make Celeste easier?"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"It lets the player adjust selected conditions of play. The important design idea is that the player chooses the route, rather than accessibility being treated as a separate game."}]]}],["$","details","What accessibility options does OutBrick have?",{"children":[["$","summary",null,{"children":"What accessibility options does OutBrick have?"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"OutBrick reinforces color with shapes and glyphs and includes a color-blind mode. We continue to treat player feedback and testing as part of the work."}]]}]]]}]
